Use the GitHub CLI to create a repository-level policy scoped to this workflow. This changes live repository settings, so run it from your terminal when you’re ready. Your account needs repository administration permission.

```sh
gh api repos/thedannywahl/pantoken/actions/policies \
  --method POST \
  --input - <<'JSON'
{
  "name": "Allow trusted automation policy events",
  "enforcement": "active",
  "conditions": {
    "workflow_path": {
      "include": [".github/workflows/renovate-policy.yml"],
      "exclude": []
    }
  },
  "rules": [
    {
      "type": "restrict_action_events",
      "parameters": {
        "allowed_events": ["pull_request_target", "pull_request_review"]
      }
    }
  ]
}
JSON
```

Verify it was created:

```sh
gh api repos/thedannywahl/pantoken/actions/policies \
  --jq '.policies[] | {name, enforcement, conditions, rules}'
```

Check that the policy is `active`, targets only `.github/workflows/renovate-policy.yml`, and allows those two events. The warning should clear on a subsequent run; it doesn’t require changing the workflow file. GitHub documents this in the [Actions policies REST API](https://docs.github.com/en/rest/actions/policies#create-a-repository-actions-policy).

I confirmed the saved policy is ID `9202`, and GitHub stored it with `conditions: null` and `rules: null`. It **doesn’t configure the event exception**, so don’t create another policy.

Try updating the existing policy with the full body:

```sh
gh api repos/thedannywahl/pantoken/actions/policies/9202 \
